= Solution
{parent=a}

For a finite <irreducible Markov chain> that is an <aperiodic Markov chain>, set
$$
d_n(t)=\max_x\|P_n^t(x,\cdot)-\pi_n\|_{\mathrm{TV}},\qquad
\boxed{t_{\mathrm{mix}}^{(n)}(\alpha)=\min\{t\in\mathbb Z_{\geq0}:d_n(t)\leq\alpha\}.}
$$
Here $\pi_n$ is its unique <stationary distribution>, and the distance is <total variation distance>. Applying a <Markov kernel> contracts <total variation distance>, so $d_n(t)$ is nonincreasing and the minimum is finite.

Use the <ratio definition of cutoff>: a family has <cutoff for Markov chains> if, for every fixed $0<\varepsilon<1/2$,
$$
\boxed{\frac{t_{\mathrm{mix}}^{(n)}(\varepsilon)}{t_{\mathrm{mix}}^{(n)}(1-\varepsilon)}\longrightarrow1,}
$$
with the denominator positive eventually. Thus the transition between any two fixed distance levels takes negligible time compared with the <mixing time> scale. By monotonicity and squeezing, this also gives $t_{\mathrm{mix}}^{(n)}(\alpha)/t_{\mathrm{mix}}^{(n)}(\beta)\to1$ for any fixed $\alpha,\beta\in(0,1)$.

Some definitions of <cutoff for Markov chains> additionally require the <mixing time> to diverge. That extra requirement cannot be built into the definition here: the final request in part (d) explicitly asks for a bounded-time counterexample. The ratio convention is the one consistent with that request.

= b
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Take the real <inner product>
$$
\langle f,g\rangle_\pi=\sum_{x\in S}\pi(x)f(x)g(x).
$$
The <stationary distribution> is strictly positive for an <irreducible Markov chain>. <Detailed balance> gives $\langle Pf,g\rangle_\pi=\langle f,Pg\rangle_\pi$, so $P$ is a <self-adjoint operator>. Choose a real <orthonormal eigenbasis> $f_1,\ldots,f_m$, where $m=|S|$, $Pf_j=\lambda_jf_j$, $f_1\equiv1$, $\lambda_1=1$, and $\pi(f_j)=0$ for $j\geq2$.

For fixed $y$, apply the <spectral decomposition> to $g_y(z)=\mathbf1_{\{y\}}(z)/\pi(y)$. Its coefficient against $f_j$ is $f_j(y)$, whereas $(P^tg_y)(x)=P^t(x,y)/\pi(y)$. Thus the <spectral kernel expansion of a reversible chain> is
$$
\boxed{\frac{P^t(x,y)}{\pi(y)}=\sum_{j=1}^{m}\lambda_j^t f_j(x)f_j(y).}
$$
This holds for all integers $t\geq0$; at $t=0$ each multiplier is one, including when $\lambda_j=0$. Negative <eigenvalues> are allowed, and retain their signs for odd $t$.

= c
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Let $m\geq2$ be the number of states and put
$$
r=\max_{2\leq j\leq m}|\lambda_j|,\qquad
\boxed{\gamma_*=1-r,\qquad t_{\mathrm{rel}}=\gamma_*^{-1}.}
$$
The <absolute L2 spectral gap of a reversible Markov chain> is $\gamma_*$; in this question the <relaxation time> means the <absolute relaxation time>. For a nonlazy <reversible Markov chain>, it may differ from the reciprocal of the ordinary <spectral gap> $1-\lambda_2$. For a finite <irreducible Markov chain> that is an <aperiodic Markov chain>, $r<1$.

For $r>0$, choose a real <eigenfunction> $f$ whose <eigenvalue> has modulus $r$, and an initial state $x$ maximizing $|f(x)|=\|f\|_\infty$. It has $\pi(f)=0$, and the definition of <total variation distance> implies
$$
r^t\|f\|_\infty=|P^tf(x)-\pi(f)|\leq2\|f\|_\infty\|P^t(x,\cdot)-\pi\|_{\mathrm{TV}}.
$$
This is the <eigenvalue lower bound for total variation mixing>, $d(t)\geq r^t/2$. At $t=t_{\mathrm{mix}}(\varepsilon)$, for $0<\varepsilon<1/2$,
$$
t\geq\frac{\log(1/(2\varepsilon))}{-\log r}\geq\frac{r}{1-r}\log\frac1{2\varepsilon}.
$$
The second inequality follows from $-r\log r\leq1-r$, the supplied inequality with $x=1-r$. Since $r/(1-r)=t_{\mathrm{rel}}-1$,
$$
\boxed{(t_{\mathrm{rel}}-1)\log\frac1{2\varepsilon}\leq t_{\mathrm{mix}}(\varepsilon).}
$$
When $r=0$ the left side is zero, so no logarithm of zero is needed. For $\varepsilon\geq1/2$ the left side is nonpositive and the conclusion is automatic. A one-state chain has <mixing time> zero; with the convention $\gamma_*=1$ the bound is again trivial.

= d
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Use the <ratio definition of cutoff> from part (a), and write $T_n=t_{\mathrm{mix}}^{(n)}(1/4)$. In the <reversible Markov chain> setting of the preceding parts let $\gamma_n=1-\lambda_{2,n}$ and $\gamma_{*,n}=1-\max_{j\geq2}|\lambda_{j,n}|$. In particular $\gamma_{*,n}\leq\gamma_n$.

If $\gamma_nT_n$ did not tend to infinity, there would be a subsequence and $B>0$ for which $\gamma_nT_n\leq B$. Along it part (c) implies, for every fixed $0<\varepsilon<1/2$,
$$
\frac{t_{\mathrm{mix}}^{(n)}(\varepsilon)}{T_n}
\geq\left(\frac1{\gamma_{*,n}T_n}-\frac1{T_n}\right)\log\frac1{2\varepsilon}
\geq\left(\frac1B-\frac1{T_n}\right)\log\frac1{2\varepsilon}.
$$
Since $T_n\to\infty$, choose fixed $\varepsilon$ so small that $\log(1/(2\varepsilon))>B$. The lower limit of this ratio is then greater than one, contradicting <cutoff for Markov chains>. Thus the <Peres product condition> holds:
$$
\boxed{\gamma_nT_n\longrightarrow\infty.}
$$
In fact the argument with $\gamma_{*,n}$ proves the stronger absolute-gap product condition. Part (d) does not repeat the <reversible Markov chain> hypothesis; the ordinary real ordering of <eigenvalues> above uses that hypothesis. If arbitrary chains are intended, define the <spectral gap> explicitly: the <eigenvalue lower bound for total variation mixing> works for complex <eigenfunctions> too, and proves the same assertion for $\gamma_*$ or for $1-\max_{j\geq2}\operatorname{Re}\lambda_j$, which is at least $\gamma_*$. No real ordering of complex <eigenvalues> is implicit.

For the requested counterexample take nonlazy <simple random walk> on the <complete graph> $K_n$, $n\geq3$. Let $\Pi$ have every row equal to the <uniform distribution on a finite set>, and let $a=-1/(n-1)$. Then
$$
P=\Pi+a(I-\Pi),\quad P^t=\Pi+a^t(I-\Pi),\quad
\boxed{d_n(t)=\left(1-\frac1n\right)(n-1)^{-t}.}
$$
Thus $d_n(0)\to1$ and $d_n(1)=1/n\to0$: every fixed-level <mixing time> is eventually one. This is a bounded-time jump satisfying the <ratio definition of cutoff>. The ordinary <spectral gap> is $n/(n-1)$ and the absolute one is $(n-2)/(n-1)$, so both products with $T_n=1$ tend to one, not infinity. The divergence assumption on $T_n$ is therefore essential under this convention.

