= a
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

On a finite state space with at least two states, as in the surrounding questions, for a real function write $\pi(f)=\sum_x\pi(x)f(x)$ and $\operatorname{Var}_\pi(f)=\sum_x\pi(x)(f(x)-\pi(f))^2$. The <Poincare inequality for a reversible Markov chain> with constant $C$ is
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{Var}_\pi(f)\leq C\mathcal E(f,f)\quad\text{for every }f,}
$$
where the <Dirichlet form of a Markov chain> is
$$
\mathcal E(f,f)=\frac12\sum_{x,y}\pi(x)P(x,y)(f(x)-f(y))^2=\langle f,(I-P)f\rangle_\pi.
$$
The associated <spectral gap> is the ordinary gap $\gamma=1-\lambda_2$, with variational formula
$$
\boxed{\gamma=\inf_{\operatorname{Var}_\pi(f)>0}\frac{\mathcal E(f,f)}{\operatorname{Var}_\pi(f)}.}
$$
Equivalently minimize $\mathcal E(f,f)$ subject to $\pi(f)=0$ and $\langle f,f\rangle_\pi=1$. Thus the <Poincare inequality for a reversible Markov chain> holds precisely for $C\geq1/\gamma$, and the optimal constant is $1/\gamma$. It controls the ordinary rather than absolute <spectral gap>, so negative <eigenvalues> do not invalidate it. If the unspecified state space in this part is infinite, the <Poincare inequality for a reversible Markov chain> and the variational infimum still make sense on nonconstant functions in $L^2(\pi)$, using the <self-adjoint operator> on that <Hilbert space>; the resulting <spectral gap> need not be represented by an actual second <eigenvalue>. The finite-state formula above is the intended specialization.

= b
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For a finite <irreducible Markov chain> satisfying <detailed balance>, define the directed-edge capacity $Q(u,v)=\pi(u)P(u,v)$. For every ordered pair $(x,y)$ choose a <path> $\eta_{xy}$ from $x$ to $y$ using only positive-capacity <edges>; take the empty <path> for $x=y$. Let $N_e(\eta)$ count occurrences of the directed <edge> $e$. The <canonical paths Poincare bound> says that
$$
\rho=\max_{e:Q(e)>0}\frac{1}{Q(e)}\sum_{x,y}\pi(x)\pi(y)|\eta_{xy}|N_e(\eta_{xy})
$$
is a valid <Poincare inequality for a reversible Markov chain> constant. Simple <paths> give $N_e\in\{0,1\}$; allowing repetitions requires the multiplicities shown.

To prove the theorem, expand the <variance> using two independent samples from $\pi$:
$$
\operatorname{Var}_\pi(f)=\frac12\sum_{x,y}\pi(x)\pi(y)(f(x)-f(y))^2.
$$
Telescoping along $\eta_{xy}$ and applying the <Cauchy-Schwarz inequality> gives
$$
(f(x)-f(y))^2\leq|\eta_{xy}|\sum_eN_e(\eta_{xy})(f(e^-)-f(e^+))^2.
$$
Interchange the finite sums, then use the definition of $\rho$:
$$
\begin{aligned}
\operatorname{Var}_\pi(f)&\leq\frac12\sum_e(f(e^-)-f(e^+))^2\sum_{x,y}\pi(x)\pi(y)|\eta_{xy}|N_e(\eta_{xy})\\
&\leq\frac\rho2\sum_eQ(e)(f(e^-)-f(e^+))^2=\rho\mathcal E(f,f).
\end{aligned}
$$
Consequently $\boxed{\operatorname{Var}_\pi(f)\leq\rho\mathcal E(f,f),\quad\gamma\geq1/\rho}$. All <edges> here are directed, so the $1/2$ in the <Dirichlet form of a Markov chain> is retained consistently.

= c
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The <lazy Markov chain> has holding probability $1/2$ and <transition probability> $1/(2n)$ to each neighbour in the <hypercube graph>. Its <stationary distribution> is $\pi(x)=2^{-n}$ by symmetry.

For $(x,y)$ choose the <path> changing the differing coordinates in increasing order. Its length is at most $n$. Fix a directed <edge> $(u,u^{(k)})$ that flips coordinate $k$. A <path> uses this <edge> exactly when $x_k=u_k$, $y_k=-u_k$, $y_j=u_j$ for $j<k$, and $x_j=u_j$ for $j>k$. The coordinates $x_j$ for $j<k$ and $y_j$ for $j>k$ are free. Thus exactly $2^{n-1}$ ordered pairs use it, while
$$
Q(u,u^{(k)})=\frac{2^{-n}}{2n},\qquad
\sum_{(x,y):e\in\eta_{xy}}\pi(x)\pi(y)=2^{n-1}2^{-2n}=2^{-n-1}.
$$
The unweighted load divided by capacity equals $n$; multiplying by the maximum length yields $\rho\leq n^2$. The <canonical paths Poincare bound> therefore proves the slightly stronger $\operatorname{Var}_\pi(f)\leq n^2\mathcal E(f,f)$, and in particular the requested
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{Var}_\pi(f)\leq2n^2\mathcal E(f,f),\qquad\gamma\geq\frac1{2n^2}.}
$$
One can keep the actual lengths: among these $2^{n-1}$ pairs, coordinate $k$ always differs, and each of the other $n-1$ coordinates differs for half the pairs. Their mean <path> length is $(n+1)/2$, giving exact congestion $\rho=n(n+1)/2$. This refinement still has order $n^2$.

= d
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

For each <subset> $J\subseteq\{1,\ldots,n\}$, take the <Walsh character> $f_J(x)=\prod_{j\in J}x_j$, including $f_\varnothing\equiv1$. Under the <uniform distribution on a finite set> these functions are an <orthonormal basis>: $f_Jf_K=f_{J\triangle K}$ has mean zero unless $J=K$, since the coordinates are independent fair signs. There are $2^n$ such functions, the full dimension of the function space.

Flipping a coordinate in $J$ changes the sign, while flipping one outside $J$ leaves the value unchanged. The total probability of a sign change is $|J|/(2n)$, so
$$
Pf_J=\left(1-\frac{|J|}{n}\right)f_J.
$$
The <spectrum of lazy random walk on the hypercube> is therefore
$$
\boxed{\lambda_k=1-\frac{k}{n}\quad\text{with multiplicity }\binom nk,\quad0\leq k\leq n.}
$$
All <eigenvalues> are nonnegative and the second largest is $1-1/n$. Hence both the ordinary <spectral gap> and the absolute <spectral gap> equal $\boxed{\gamma=1/n}$. The requested bound in part (c) is smaller than the exact gap by a factor $2n$; even the refined increasing-coordinate <canonical paths Poincare bound> loses a factor $(n+1)/2$. The true optimal <Poincare inequality for a reversible Markov chain> constant is $n$.

